      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the possible degrees of the remainder when a polynomial is divided by \\(2x^2 - 7x + 18\\), we need to understand the theorem for polynomial division. According to the polynomial division algorithm, if a polynomial \\(P(x)\\) is divided by a non-zero polynomial \\(D(x)\\), the division can be expressed as:\n\n\\[P(x) = Q(x) \\cdot D(x) + R(x),\\]\n\nwhere \\(Q(x)\\) is the quotient and \\(R(x)\\) is the remainder. The degree of the remainder \\(R(x)\\) must be less than the degree of the divisor \\(D(x)\\). In this problem, the divisor is \\(2x^2 - 7x + 18\\), which is a polynomial of degree 2. Therefore, the possible degrees of the remainder \\(R(x)\\) must be less than 2. This means the possible degrees of \\(R(x)\\) are 0 or 1.\n\nTo confirm, let's consider the degrees of the terms in the divisor. The divisor is \\(2x^2 - 7x + 18\\), and the degree of the highest term is 2. The degree of the remainder must be less than the degree of the divisor, so the remainder can be a constant (degree 0) or a linear term (degree 1).\n\nThus, the possible degrees of the remainder are \\(\\boxed{0, 1}\\).",
